          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"tolstoy-war-and-peace-84d11ba0fe45ba21\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"Sonya directly avows that she loves Nikolai, explicitly characterizing that love as sibling-like. The continuation that she will always love him gives slight undertaking-like force, but the highlighted core is principally a presentation of her enduring emotional state. Nothing indicates that the words escape as a reflexive affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The context leaves some uncertainty about whether the fraternal characterization fully represents Sonya’s romantic feelings and whether “always will” is a promise or merely a prediction. Neither uncertainty changes the utterance’s dominant truth-conditional force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Sonya directly addresses Nikolai during their private conversation: “Я люблю вас, как брата” (“I love you as a brother”).\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Sonya has refused Dolokhov after saying she loves someone else, evidently Nikolai. Nikolai admits love but explicitly promises nothing and urges her to reconsider Dolokhov. Sonya’s response rejects material or marital demands while affirming an enduring, ostensibly sibling-like love. Her formulation may protect her from Nikolai’s lack of commitment or accommodate his terms, but such possible self-protection concerns the avowal’s sincerity or precise content, not its core semantic mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Я люблю вас, как брата, и всегда буду любить” (“I love you as a brother, and I always shall love you”).\",\"supports\":\"A direct present avowal strongly supports T; the future continuation supplies limited support for an enduring relational undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Sonya has refused Dolokhov, while Natasha reports that Sonya said she loved someone else.\",\"supports\":\"Identifies Nikolai as the emotionally significant addressee and supports reading the utterance as a meaningful avowal rather than an empty formula.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Nikolai says, “я ничего не обещаю” (“I promise nothing”) and asks Sonya to consider Dolokhov’s proposal; Sonya answers that she wants nothing and needs nothing more.\",\"supports\":\"The exchange foregrounds Nikolai’s noncommitment and makes Sonya’s response chiefly an affirmation of love without a demand for reciprocal commitment, limiting P.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Sonya’s statement is a coherent, deliberate reply in an extended private discussion; the narration does not describe it as involuntarily blurted or reflexively produced.\",\"supports\":\"Supports E=0 despite the scene’s strong emotion and Sonya’s subsequent joy.\",\"confidence\":0.95}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the direct avowal, with slight P support from the declaration of enduring love. Emotional intensity and later joy remain contextual and do not require E; no important aspect of the core force falls outside T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The translation was treated only as an analytical aid; evidential claims are grounded in the supplied Russian passage.\"}"
        }
